WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.460
<v Instructor>Now let's look at AWS Control Tower.</v>

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.590
So it's an easy way for you to set up

3
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.090
and govern a secure and compliant

4
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.660
multi-account AWS environments based on best practices.

5
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.550
So out of it, you're going to get

6
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.220
your environment automated in just a few clicks.

7
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.410
You're going to have ongoing policy management

8
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.600
using guardrails and we see this in this lecture.

9
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.190
We're going to detect policy violations

10
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.110
and remediate them automatically.

11
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.520
And we can monitor compliance

12
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.680
through an interactive dashboard.

13
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.870
So Control Tower is a way for you to really automate

14
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.340
best practice multi-accounts

15
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.980
and it runs on top of AWS organizations.

16
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.870
So it's going to set up an organization for you

17
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.700
and then organize these accounts and implement

18
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.510
automatically the service control policies you need.

19
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.220
So out of it there is the account factory

20
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.760
and since you automate account provisioning and deployments

21
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.910
and it allows you to create pre-approved baseline

22
00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.610
and configuration options for all the accounts

23
00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.710
in your organization, such as, for example,

24
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.830
configuring the default VPC, configuring the subnets,

25
00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.990
the regions and so on.

26
00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.200
And to do so is going to use the underlying service

27
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.640
called the AWS Service Catalog

28
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.200
to provision these new accounts.

29
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.210
So let's have a look in where an account factor

30
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.110
will be very helpful.

31
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.510
So we have an cloud computing environments

32
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.220
and then we have a data center

33
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.850
with an ADFS and an active directory.

34
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.380
And now we are going to establish a VPN

35
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.310
or direct connect connection

36
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.160
between the cloud and the corporate data center.

37
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.890
And so therefore when we use Control Tower

38
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.380
and a lending zone and we create accounts

39
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.520
for account factory,

40
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.180
then at the center of it is going to be IAM Identity Center.

41
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.650
And we can set up in different ways.

42
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:32.910
But if you wanted to integrate

43
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.280
with Microsoft Active Directory

44
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.080
on your corporate data center

45
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.860
then you would create an AWS managed Microsoft AD,

46
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.060
which would be the source

47
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.670
of notification for your IAM Identity Center.

48
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.070
And then you would create a two-way trust

49
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.870
between active directory

50
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.720
on your corporate data center and AWS.

51
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.040
From there, any accounts created through your landing zone

52
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.680
and through account factory will be properly

53
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.080
configured to leverage authentication

54
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.220
through IAM ID Center and therefore use underlyingly

55
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.110
the active directory you have in the cloud

56
00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.023
and the one you have also on your corporate data center.

57
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.300
Now there's another thing

58
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.130
with Control Tower called guardrails.

59
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.710
And this is used to detect and remediate policy violations.

60
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.080
So it's going to give you ongoing governance.

61
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.240
And then you have two kinds of guardrails.

62
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.060
You have preventive guardrails or use SCPs.

63
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.400
And this is, for example, to have an action

64
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.250
to disable or disallow the creation

65
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.100
of access keys from the root user.

66
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.000
That's one way.

67
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.830
Or detective where you want to give

68
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.030
all permissions to your accounts,

69
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.670
but then you use config to detect, for example,

70
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.360
whether or not MFA is enabled for the root user and so on.

71
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.680
And config will be very helpful

72
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.180
because it gives you compliance

73
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.670
or non-compliance of resources.

74
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:48.810
And so here's an example.

75
00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.080
We, for example, want to see which resources are untagged.

76
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.560
So we have a detective guardrail in Control Tower

77
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.450
using config and it's going to monitor

78
00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.850
for untagged resources in your member accounts

79
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.250
as part of your organization is going to trigger

80
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.560
if it's noncompliance and SNS topic,

81
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.350
which can notify an admin, maybe invoke Lambda function

82
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.450
and that Lambda function itself can remediate

83
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.880
to this and add tag where needed.

84
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.700
So there are a few guardrail levels for Control Tower.

85
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.410
There is the mandatory guardrail,

86
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.820
which is automatically enabled

87
00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.430
and enforced by AWS Control Tower.

88
00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.380
So for example, you want to disallow

89
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.870
public read access to the log archive accounts

90
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.820
and this is mandatory.

91
00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.180
Strongly recommended, which is based on AWS practices.

92
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.340
And for example, you wanna say you want to enable

93
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.070
EBS volumes attached to EC2 instances.

94
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.020
And then you have elective guardrails,

95
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.460
which is commonly used by enterprises.

96
00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.260
This is optional as well, for example,

97
00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.460
to disallow delete actions without MFA in S3 buckets.

98
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.230
Okay, so that's it for Control Tower.

99
00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.310
I hope you liked it.

100
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.260
And I will see you in the next lecture.

